I suppose you're right ...

this isn't easy. Y'see, I'm not trying to unbalance the game. On the
contrary, I'm trying to add a bit of challenge to it by creating a sort
of double-edged artifact. It comes with a whopping set of advantages,
coupled with a set of huge disadvantages.    The idea being to put a
person in the position where they can take the artifact, gain some
super advantages from it -- but also pay a serious price for it.   Or,
on the contrary, you can just ignore the weapon and spare yourself much
pain and heartache -- but that also means you don't get the advantages.


Which would it be?  Take the power, and the curse that goes with it?
Or avoid the curse -- and the power?

I was thinking that the weapon would basically make one the enemy of
the gods.  That to take it meant having to follow the path of a
Siegfried, or perhaps an Alberich, where one cuts one's way through the
world without the gods and in defiance of them, by availing oneself of
forbidden power.  This power gives abilities no other mortal man has ..
at the expense of making divine enemies no other mortal man has.

So the weapon balances itself. It brings great advantages and great
disadvantages.  Whether it's worth the trouble it brings is up to the
individual player, and their playing style.

I also think of it as a sort of trainer for the AOY.  The Amulet,
in-game, is a net negative, as it confers massive disadvantages without
corresponding advantages. No one would carry the blasted thing if it
wasn't necessary to win the game.   By contrast, this artifact should
confer some of the disadvantages of the AOY so the player can get used
to them, while at the same time conferring advantages so one isn't
simply burdened with a millstone.

Also, someone else mentioned that the Tokugawa weren't exactly lawful.
Well, yeah, but this is story-telling, not history :).  The Knights of
history (IIRC) weren't especially devoted to chivalry either, but we
still punish knights in game for, e.g., striking a running opponent.
And from a story-telling perspective, from about 1650 to 1860 the
Tokugawa were the embodiment of Law in Japan.  So it makes sense that
they would be lawful. And if the weapon is a hellblade, it has to be
chaotic, for the simple reason that there aren't any cursed lawful or
neutral weapons in nethack.  

